-- title:
--   Admissibly convex domains are filled
-- statement:
--   A finite vertex set $D$ is admissibly convex if there are integers $\ell_k<h_k$ ($k=0,1,2$) such that $v\in D$ exactly when $\ell_k<t_k(v)<h_k$ for the three tiling coordinates $t_0=2y/\sqrt3$, $t_1=x-y/\sqrt3+\frac12$, $t_2=x+y/\sqrt3+\frac12$ of the position $(x,y)$ of $v$. The star faces of $D$ are the hexagons adjacent to some vertex of $D$; following the boundary of this face set with the star faces on the right gives the star boundary walk. A domain $D$ is filled if every polygon whose vertices lie in $D$ has all its interior vertices in $D$. Every admissibly convex domain is filled:
--   $$
--   P\in\mathrm{polygons}(D)\implies\mathrm{interior}(P)\subseteq D .
--   $$
--
--   Convex domains have no holes, so loops inside them enclose only domain vertices.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/HoneycombCorridor/ConvexDomains.lean#L72-L87

